#    169:   2486: !
#    169:   2487: ! {*******************************************************************************
#    169:   2488: !
#    169:   2489: ! Execute routine by number
#    169:   2490: !
#    169:   2491: ! Given a routine number, executes that routine. All of the input parameters are
#    169:   2492: ! passed on the stack, and the result, if any, also returned on the stack.
#    169:   2493: !
#    169:   2494: ! All of the input parameters are removed from the stack, leaving just the result
#    169:   2495: ! (if any).
#    169:   2496: !
#    169:   2497: ! The load of parameters is fairly ad-hoc. Value parameters are simply fetched.
#    169:   2498: ! VAR and VIEW pameters have to be loaded into a buffer to transfer.
#    169:   2499: !
#    169:   2500: ! The layout of the parameters and return are:
#    169:   2501: !
#    169:   2502: ! params+intsize*2: return value
#    169:   2503: ! params+intsize:   param1
#    169:   2504: ! params:           param2
#    169:   2505: ! ...
#    169:   2506: !
#    169:   2507: ! ie., the parameters start at the params address, then climb upwards until the
#    169:   2508: ! return value (if any). The params address should be incremented past all
#    169:   2509: ! parameters, but left pointing at the return value, if any. Otherwise params
#    169:   2510: ! is just left above all parameters.
#    169:   2511: !
#    169:   2512: ! *******************************************************************************}
